Case-insensitive prefix matching on Windows in `hasNormalizedRootPrefix` uses `strings.ToLower` on both path and root, but `normalizeInstallRoot` applies `filepath.EvalSymlinks` which resolves to the canonical case from the filesystem. If the SCOOP env var is set to `D:\Tools` but the actual directory on disk is `D:\tools`, EvalSymlinks will resolve to the filesystem's canonical case, then ToLower comparison will succeed. However, if the filesystem is mounted case-sensitive (WSL2, ReFS with case-sensitivity enabled), EvalSymlinks returns the provided case when the path doesn't exist on disk, causing `normalizeInstallRoot("D:\\Tools")` to preserve capital-T while `normalizedExecPath()` independently resolves the binary's location to lowercase, breaking the match. The fallback to `cleaned` on EvalSymlinks error (line 401-402) makes this path reachable. Test coverage exists for case-insensitive matching (line 1570-1577) but doesn't cover the EvalSymlinks-error case where case diverges.
