Yes. Most of the obfuscation is the name `locate`, the vague `rest`, and the index arithmetic. The same mechanism reads cleanly with `strings.CutPrefix` and `strings.Cut`, which already return exactly the "remainder plus matched" pair:

```go
// pathUnderRoot reports whether execPath lives under one of the probe's roots
// or markers and returns the part of the path below that root, e.g.
// "cli/current/entire.exe" for a Scoop install.
func (p installProbe) pathUnderRoot(execPath string) (string, bool) {
	if p.roots != nil {
		for _, raw := range p.roots() {
			root := normalizeInstallRoot(raw)
			if root == "" {
				continue
			}
			if below, ok := strings.CutPrefix(execPath, root); ok {
				return below, true
			}
		}
	}
	for _, marker := range p.markers {
		if _, below, ok := strings.Cut(execPath, marker); ok {
			return below, true
		}
	}
	return "", false
}
```

The caller then says what it means:

```go
for _, p := range installProbes {
	if below, ok := p.pathUnderRoot(path); ok {
		return p.command(below, currentVersion)
	}
}
```

and Scoop's command reads as a sentence: `if scoopAppName(pathBelowRoot) == "cli"`, where `scoopAppName` is "first path segment".

Two further options if the unused parameter on brew and mise still bothers you:

- **Make the field a `string` instead of a func for brew and mise.** They return constants, so `command func(...)` could become `command string` plus an optional `commandFor func(below, version string) string` that only Scoop and brew set. That adds a second field and a nil check, so I would not do it.
- **Give the struct a doc comment that explains the contract once**, so nobody has to infer it from the signature. That is cheap and I would do it regardless.

If you want, I will apply the rename plus the `CutPrefix`/`Cut` rewrite and the doc comment. It changes no behavior and the tests cover it.

